Cactus Code Thorn Geodesic
Author(s)    : Yuhua Li <liyhts@yeah.net>
Maintainer(s): Yuhua Li <liyhts@yeah.net>
Licence      : GPLv2+.  See the COPYING file for full license terms and
               conditions.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Purpose

This thorn integrates time-like geodesics for test particles through a
spacetime carried on the Cactus grid.  The metric is sampled from the
grid at the particle position, the geodesic equation is integrated with
GSL Runge-Kutta in that sampled metric, and the mass-shell constraint
g(u,u) = -1 is re-imposed after every step.  Because the metric comes
from the grid, the thorn works on any numerical spacetime, not only on
analytic backgrounds.

Four interchangeable metric samplers are selected by
Geodesic::metric_interp: "l27" and "local27" (local polynomial fits to
the grid metric, with 6th-order finite-difference Christoffel symbols),
"aei" (AEILocalInterp pointwise Lagrange interpolation) and "carpet" /
"carpetx" (grid-carried interpolation).  "Exact" evaluates the analytic
Kerr-Schild metric instead, which provides a reference run independent
of the grid.

The thorn supports Carpet, CarpetX and CarpetRegrid2 multi-level AMR,
CarpetX multi-patch grids, MPI (owner-computes particle distribution
with collective re-owning at chunk boundaries) and OpenMP, restart from
checkpoints, and writes a per-particle ASCII dump

    t x y z ut ux uy uz tau cctk_time

2. Documentation

doc/documentation.tex is the ThornGuide document; README.md is the
detailed user guide, including the numerical pitfalls found during
development; doc/par/ holds the parameter files that reproduce every
number in the accompanying paper.  par/example.par is a complete,
tested example that runs on a stock Einstein Toolkit checkout.

3. Tests

test/ contains regression tests declared in test/test.ccl.  They are
regression tests (bit-comparison of the particle dump against reference
data), not convergence or physics tests.  test/check_mpi_identity.sh
additionally checks that the particle dump is bit-identical at 1, 2 and
4 MPI ranks.

4. Known limitations

- Particle state is stored in DISTRIB=CONSTANT replicated grid arrays,
  which is appropriate for the hundreds to tens of thousands of
  particles this thorn is designed for, not for arbitrarily large
  ensembles.
- In AMR setups with more than one MPI rank, a residual dependence of
  the sampled metric on the owning rank remains under investigation;
  unigrid runs are bit-identical across rank counts (see README.md).

